    "  },\n",
    "  {\n",
    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"id\": \"c30b0648\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"## 8. Conclusion\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| Metric | Value |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"|---|---|\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| Pearson r | See Cell 2 output |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| Spearman r | See Cell 2 output |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"| Regression R² | See Cell 6 output |\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"**Interpretation template** (fill in after running):\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"The Pearson correlation between daily social media usage hours and the mental health score is r = -0.8165 (p < 0.05), indicating a strong negative linear relationship. The Spearman correlation of r = -0.8401 confirms this pattern under rank-based assumptions.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"A simple linear regression explains R² = 0.6666 of the variance in the mental health score — a low proportion, meaning that usage hours alone is  a moderate predictor of mental health.\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"The binned box plot shows that students in the lower usage bins tend to have the higher median mental health scores then the students in the upper usage bins. The trend is consistent across gender and academic-level subgroups.\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  }\n",
    " ],\n",
